WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:06.380
But before I show you an actual phase
diagram, I want to just take a moment

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.940
and try to explain to
you how this can be.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.876
And we're going to do this by
just drawing little pictures

00:00:12.876 --> 00:00:14.750
and just thinking to
ourselves, how could you

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:17.855
imagine packing fats together?

00:00:17.855 --> 00:00:20.480
So remember, I told you that
triglycerides-- and this is really

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.270
a general fact for all fats-- but
triglycerides actually consist

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:26.030
of these two arms, with this
one long arm that's stuck to it.

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:29.374
And if we start to think to ourselves,
just draw on a little doodle pad,

00:00:29.374 --> 00:00:31.790
how is it that we're going to
stack these things together,

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.650
there are really at least
two ways that you can do it.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:38.510
So one is that what you could
imagine doing is taking the long arm

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.750
and stacking it in such a way, like
it's shown in this picture, where

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:45.380
the long arm is actually--
that is, the single arm,

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:48.740
is actually adjacent to the double
arms in the layer before it.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.500
So this picture is what's
called double chain packing.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.130
What you see is that the
long arm of one molecule

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:57.562
is on top of the double
arm on the other.

00:00:57.562 --> 00:01:00.020
And they basically flip back
and forth from layer to layer,

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.760
which gives rise to the packing.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.280
So there's also another
logical possibility,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.580
which is what people call the triple
chain packing, in which what you do

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.589
is you put the two double
arms on top of each other,

00:01:10.589 --> 00:01:12.630
and you put the single
arms on top of each other.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:17.060
So you basically align the things so
that the single arms are like this,

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:18.890
and the double arms are like this.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:20.220
That's shown in this picture.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.594
And when you do that, that's
called triple chain packing.

00:01:22.594 --> 00:01:25.880
And that's a different way
of packing the triglycerides.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.600
Now, these two different
packings have different energies.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.850
It might be more sticky
to do double chain

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:33.710
or more sticky to do triple chain.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:36.620
And whether it is,
what is true, actually,

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.607
really depends on the
details of the fats.

00:01:39.607 --> 00:01:42.690
I mean, so for example, if you could
just imagine as a thought experiment,

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.740
if you had a saturated
fat in the single chain,

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.380
then the saturated fats might
like-- the single chains might

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:53.114
like to stack together very well,
whereas if the unsaturated fats were

00:01:53.114 --> 00:01:55.280
in the chains, then they're
sort of harder to stick.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.280
And then, of course, the heads
with the two double chains,

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.460
that could be a different packing--
stacking interaction all together.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.890
And it really, as you can
see from listening to me,

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:07.562
it gets a bit complicated to figure out,
just from thinking about it alone, what

00:02:07.562 --> 00:02:09.020
the different stacking behavior is.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.900
And this is really something
which is an experimental subject.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.650
It can be empirically determined
by studying the phases of fats

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:19.430
and by looking under the microscope,
under a very powerful microscope,

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:22.310
to find out how the
molecules are stacked.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:24.890
OK so with that as
background, I now want

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.780
to show you the phase
diagram of chocolate.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.180
And I'm going to try to relate
this to what I just said.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.550
So this is the phase
diagram of chocolate.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:35.480
So chocolate has, as I
said, six crystalline phases

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:39.170
that vary in temperature from
18 degrees to 36 degrees.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:43.010
So the lowest phase, which
people call phase 1, cleverly,

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.380
has a melting temperature
of about 18 degrees.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.912
The second phase, which people
call phase 2, is about 22 degrees.

00:02:48.912 --> 00:02:50.245
And then there are other phases.

00:02:50.245 --> 00:02:53.060
There's phase 4, which is 34
degrees, and phase 6, which

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.230
is 36 degrees, and so on and so forth.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.200
And these are the six different phases.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.749
So what do I mean by different phase?

00:02:58.749 --> 00:03:01.790
What I mean is, well, for one thing,
it melts at a different temperature.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.260
It just comes apart.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.641
If you made the material
out of only that phase,

00:03:05.641 --> 00:03:07.640
and you heated it up to
the melting temperature,

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.815
then it would come apart
at that temperature.

00:03:09.815 --> 00:03:12.440
And I also mean that if you were
to look at the structure, that

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.420
is, the way the molecules
are packed, they're

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.461
actually different in each of these
different phases, which is the reason

00:03:17.461 --> 00:03:21.347
that it melts at different positions.

00:03:21.347 --> 00:03:23.180
Now, what's interesting
about these numbers,

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:26.138
if you just think about my little
experiment with my piece of chocolate

00:03:26.138 --> 00:03:30.590
a little while ago, is that if you
remember that body temperature-- that

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:32.300
is, your mouth is 37 degrees.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:35.150
And your hand is-- let's say
it's 28 degrees or something.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:39.500
If you want to find a material
out of this phase diagram, which

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.750
doesn't melt in your hand
but melts in your mouth,

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:44.900
you can see, really, the only options
you've got, even from the get-go

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.950
are phases 5 and 6, the two
highest phases, maybe phase 4.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:50.560
It's close to the borderline.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.000
But phases 5 and 6 are
really what you have.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:00.140
And in fact, it turns out that 5
and 6, and in particular, phase 5,

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.255
is the phase that you
need to hit in order

00:04:02.255 --> 00:04:04.130
to make the chocolate
that you know and love,

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:08.182
and that we saw so spectacularly
illustrated by Henrique.

00:04:08.182 --> 00:04:09.890
Now, if you think
about preparations, you

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:11.990
can immediately see from
just looking at this diagram

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:13.906
and thinking about how
it is you're ever going

00:04:13.906 --> 00:04:16.990
to do this why it is so
hard for the home chef,

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:20.839
or for somebody who's inexperienced like
me, to make chocolate in their kitchen.

00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:24.290
It's because you somehow have to
basically get the cocoa butter-- which

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:27.627
you can buy cocoa butter, or you can
just take chocolate and melt it, like,

00:04:27.627 --> 00:04:29.960
put it at very high temperatures,
and you will basically

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.570
get for yourself a mix of cocoa butter.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:36.860
But then, the question is, how do you
get it to only crystallize in the phase

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.740
at phase 5, which is what you want?

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:44.240
I mean, clearly, if you cool it to
a low temperature like 22 degrees,

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:48.650
then it can cool into all
of these different phases.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:52.650
So what are you supposed
to do to make this happen?

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:58.810
And the complexities of making chocolate
are all associated with this issue.

